  "question": {
    "text": "If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></math> are the input voltages (either <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> is the output voltage then the two gates represented in the following circuits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>A</mi></mfenced></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>B</mi></mfenced></math> are :<br /><img src=\"assets\\qt0105_e9350ebaec5c.png\" style=\"width: 680px; height: 325px;\" />"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "OR and NOT Gate",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "AND and NOT Gate",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "AND and OR Gate",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "NAND and NOR Gate",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\st0105_12a982092d87.png\" style=\"width: 232px; height: 156px;\" /><br />In the above circuit is we are applying high voltage in any one of the diodes or in both diodes simultaneously&nbsp;then we get a high out due to forward biasing of diode while we are applied low voltage in both the diode then we get low voltage so this is like OR gate.&nbsp;</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0105_26176766e018.png\" style=\"width: 321px; height: 278px;\" /></p><p>In the above circuit which is an NPN transistor in a common emitter configuration having a phase difference of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>&nbsp;between input voltage and output voltage, when we are applied high voltage then we get low voltage vice-versa so,&nbsp;the circuit is the same as NOT gate.</p>"
